===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰer-}}
{{dercat|en|ang|gmw-pro|gem-pro|ine-pro}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from Late {{inh|en|enm|birth|t=(''nautical'') bearing away or off, clearance, berth}}.<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=birth|pos=n|id=MED4758}}</ref> Further etymology {{uncertain|en|nocap=1}}, but probably from {{m|enm|beren|t=to carry (away), bear}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=bēren|pos=''v.''(1)|noformat=1|id=MED4156}}</ref><ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1546667156|date=September 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> + {{m|enm|-th|pos={{glossary|suffix}} denoting a condition, quality, state of being, etc., forming nouns}};<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=-th(e|pos=n|id=MED45078}}</ref> if so, the English word is analysable as {{affix|en|bear|id1=convey|-th|pos2=suffix forming nouns from verbs|id2=abstract nominal}}, and is a {{piecewise doublet|en|birth|nocap=1}}.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=''v.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=3758144072|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} ''Chiefly in'' '''[[wide berth]]''': a [[sufficient#Adjective|sufficient]] [[space#Noun|space]] in the [[water#Noun|water]] for a [[ship#Noun|ship]] or other [[vessel#Noun|vessel]] to [[lie#Verb|lie]] [[at anchor]] or [[maneuver#Verb|manoeuvre]] without [[get in the way|getting in the way]] of other vessels, or [[collide|colliding]] into [[rock#Noun|rocks]] or the [[shore#Noun|shore]].
## {{senseid|en|vessel place}}{{lb|en|by extension}} A [[place#Noun|place]] for a vessel to lie at anchor or to [[moor#Verb|moor]].
##* {{RQ:Fielding Lisbon|page=191|passage={{smallcaps|Tho'}} vve vvere again got near our harbour by three in the afternoon, yet it ſeemed to require a full hour or more, before vve could come to our former place of anchoring, or '''birth''', as the captain called it.}}
##* {{RQ:Austen Mansfield Park|edition=2nd|volume=III|chapter=VII|page=151|passage="{{...|nospace=1}} She lays close to the Endymion, between her and the Cleopatra, just to the eastward of the sheer hulk." / "Ha!" cried William, "''that's'' just where I should have put her myself. It's the best '''birth''' at Spithead.{{nb...}}"}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=The Decanter|pages=493–494|pageref=493|passage=The voyage was a skilful and lucky one; and returning to her '''berth''' with her hold full of the precious sperm, the Amelia's example was soon followed by other ships, English and American, and thus the vast Sperm Whale grounds of the Pacific were thrown open.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[room#Noun|room]] in a vessel in which the [[officer#Noun|officers]] or [[company#Noun|company]] {{l|en|mess#Verb 2|t=[[eat#Verb|eat]] together}} and [[reside]]; also, a room or other place in a vessel for [[storage#Noun|storage]].
##* {{RQ:Smollett Roderick Random|volume=I|chapter=I am Reduced to Great Misery—Assaulted on Tower-hill by a Press-gang, who Put Me on Board a Tender—My Usage there—My Arrival on Board of the Thunder Man of War,{{nb...}}|page=226|passage=And vvhen he had ſhevvn me their '''birth''' (as he called it) I vvas filled vvith aſtoniſhment and horror.—VVe deſcended by divers ladders to a ſpace as dark as a dungeon, vvhich I underſtood vvas immerſed ſeveral feet under vvater, being immediately above the hold: I had no ſooner approached this diſmal gulph, than my noſe vvas ſaluted vvith an intolerable ſtench of putrified cheeſe, and rancid butter, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Marryat Midshipman|volume=I|pages=141–142|pageref=142|passage=But not only was this the first day that Jack may be said to have appeared in the service, but it was the first day in which he had entered the midshipman's '''berth''', and was made acquainted with his messmates.}}
## {{senseid|en|vessel bed}}{{lb|en|by extension}} A place on a vessel to [[sleep#Verb|sleep]], especially a [[bed#Noun|bed]] on the [[side#Noun|side]] of a [[cabin#Noun|cabin]].
##* {{RQ:Byron Letters and Journals|volume=I|chapter=Letter XXXVI. To Mr. Hodgson.|date=30 June 1809|section=stanza 3|page=191|passage=Passengers their '''births''' are clapt in, / Some to grumble, some to spew. / 'Hey day! call you that a cabin? / Why 'tis hardly three feet square; / Not enough to stow {{w|Queen Mab}} in— / Who the deuce can harbour there?'}}
##* {{RQ:Dana Two Years|chapter=XXX|page=367|passage=Just at this moment, in crossing the forecastle, one of the men saw a light below, and looking down the scuttle, saw the watch all out of their '''berths''', and afoul of one poor fellow, dragging him out of his '''berth''', and shaking him, to wake him out of a nightmare.}}
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=The Sermon|page=48|passage=All dressed and dusty as he is, Jonah throws himself into his '''berth''', and finds the little state-room ceiling almost resting on his forehead. {{...}} The lamp alarms and frightens Jonah; as lying in his '''berth''' his tormented eyes roll round the place, and this thus far successful fugitive finds no refuge for his restless glance.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=&#91;{{w|Alice Kipling}}&#93;|title=The Haunted Cabin|journal=[[w:Civil and Military Gazette|Quartette, the Christmas Annual of the Civil & Military Gazette]]|location=Lahore, British India|publisher=The “Civil and Military Gazette” Press|month=December|year=1885|page=41|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/quartetteDec1885/page/n53/mode/1up|oclc=177744251|passage=By what I then thought to be great good luck I had succeeded in getting a three-'''berth''' cabin for myself and my little boy alone—Nos. 45, 46, 47—on the starboard side of the ship.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[job#Noun|job]] or [[position#Noun|position]] on a vessel.
##* {{RQ:Dana Two Years|chapter=XXIII|page=247|passage=He was now a temperate man for life, and capable of filling any '''berth''' in a ship, and many a high station there is on shore which is held by a meaner man.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}}:
## An [[assigned#Adjective|assigned]] place for a [[person#Noun|person]] in {{lb|en|chiefly|historical}} a [[horse-drawn]] [[coach#Noun|coach]] or other [[means of transportation]], or {{lb|en|military}} in a [[barracks#Noun|barracks]].
##* {{RQ:Scott Antiquary|volume=I|chapter=I|pages=6–7|pageref=6|passage=[W]ith worldly wisdom, the first comer hastens to secure the best '''birth''' in the coach for himself, and to make the most convenient arrangement for his baggage before the arrival of his competitor.}}
## {{senseid|en|bed}} A [[bunk#Noun|bunk]] or other bed for sleeping on in a [[caravan#Noun|caravan]], a [[train#Noun|train]], etc.
##* {{RQ:Rinehart Man in Lower Ten|page=27|passage=Some passengers boarded the train there and I heard a woman's low tones, a southern voice, rich and full. Then quiet again. Every nerve was tense: time passed, perhaps ten minutes, possibly half an hour. Then, without the slightest warning, as the train rounded a curve, a heavy body was thrown into my '''berth'''.}}
## {{lb|en|road transport}} A place for a [[vehicle#Noun|vehicle]] on [[land#Noun|land]] to [[park#Verb|park]].
# {{lb|en|figurative}}:
## {{senseid|en|position}} An [[appointment]], job, or position, especially one [[regard#Verb|regarded]] as [[comfortable#Adjective|comfortable]] or [[good#Adjective|good]].
##* {{RQ:Defoe Captain Singleton|page=186|passage=He vvas a Surgeon, and they called him Doctor; but he vvas not employed in the Sloop as a Surgeon, but vvas going to ''Berbadoes'' to get a '''''Birth''''', as the Sailors call it.}}
##* {{RQ:Burney Evelina|volume=I|letter=XVI|lettername=Evelina to the Rev. Mr. Villars|page=103|passage=[Y]ou have got a good vvarm '''birth''' here; but vve ſhall beat up your quarters. Here, Lucy, Moll, come to the fire, and dry your trumpery.}}
##* {{RQ:Stowe Uncle Tom's Cabin|volume=I|chapter=An Evening in Uncle Tom's Cabin|page=53|passage=Howsomever, I&nbsp;'ll do the very best I can in gettin' Tom a good '''berth'''; as to my treatin' on him bad, you need n't be a grain afeard.}}
##* {{RQ:Thackeray Newcomes|volume=II|chapter=Belisarius|page=309|passage=How glad I am that he has got a snug '''berth''' in the City! His company really prospers, I am happy to think, unlike some companies you know of, Pen.}}
## ''Chiefly in'' '''wide berth''': a sufficient space for manoeuvring or [[safety]].
##* {{RQ:Scott Demonology|page=386|passage=The road was very narrow, with no opportunity of giving the apparent phantom what seamen call a wide '''birth'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Thackeray Newcomes|volume=II|chapter=In which Kinsmen Fall Out|page=150|passage=Sir Barnes Newcome, for fear of consequences that I should deplore, I recommend you to keep a wide '''berth''' of me, sir.}}
##* {{RQ:Morris Earthly Paradise|parts=I & II|page=17|passage=[W]e / Thought it but wise to keep the open sea / And give to warring lands a full wide '''berth'''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Burroughs Return of Tarzan|version=Burt|chapter=What Happened in the Rue Maule|page=34|passage=Tarzan had been wont to traverse the Rue Maule on his way home at night. Because it was very quiet and very dark it reminded him more of his beloved African jungle than did the noisy and garish streets surrounding it. If you are familiar with your Paris you will recall the narrow, forbidding precincts of the Rue Maule. If you are not, you need but ask the police about it to learn that in all Paris there is no street to which you should give a wider '''berth''' after dark.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|nautical|slang}} A [[proper#Adjective|proper]] place for a [[thing]].
##* {{RQ:Defoe Great Britain|volume=I|chapter=Letter III. Containing a Description of the County of Kent, &c.|pages=140–&#91;141&#93;|pageref=140|passage=[T]he Maſter-builders appoint the VVorking or Converting, as they call it, of every Piece of Timber, and give to the other Head-vvorkmen or Foremen, their Moulds for the ſquaring and cutting out of every Piece, and placing it in its proper '''Byrth''' (ſo they call it) in the Ship that is in Building; {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|sports}}:
### A position or [[seed#Noun|seed]] in a [[tournament]] [[bracket#Noun|bracket]].
###* {{quote-web|en|url=https://www.uslchampionship.com/news_article/show/1365504|work=USL Championship|passage=Rhode Island turned possession over 11 times in the attacking third, a season high in the Championship overall, and that turned into scoring opportunities. While there will be bigger challenges ahead starting this weekend away at FC Tulsa, this blueprint was a confidence-booster for the side as it pursues another postseason '''berth'''.|title=11 Numbers That Defined Week 25 in the USL Championship {{!}} Talking Points|date=2026-08-24|author=USLChampionship.com Staff|accessdate=2026-08-25|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261005134524/https://www.uslchampionship.com/news_article/show/1365504|archivedate=5 October 2026}}
### A position on a [[field of play]].
###* {{RQ:Guardian|author=[[w:Paul Doyle (journalist)|Paul Doyle]]|title=[[w:Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]]'s Theo Walcott hits hat-trick in thrilling victory over [[w:Newcastle F.C.|Newcastle]]|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220816135007/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2012/dec/29/arsenal-newcastle-united-premier-league|date=29 December 2012|passage={{w|Olivier Giroud}} then entered the fray and [[w:Theo Walcott|{{quote-gloss|Theo}} Walcott]] reverted to his more familiar '''berth''' on the right wing, quickly creating his side's fifth goal by crossing for Giroud to send a plunging header into the net from close range.}}

