# {{lb|en|transitive}}:
## {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[bring#Verb|bring]] (a [[ship#Noun|ship]] or other [[vessel#Noun|vessel]]) into a [[berth#Noun|berth]] ''(noun {{senseno|en|vessel place}})''; also, to [[provide]] a berth for (a vessel).
##: {{antonyms|en|unberth}}
##: {{coordinate terms|en|dock|moor}}
##* {{RQ:Pepys Diary|volume=VI|date=30 June 1667|page=406|passage="The Henery," being let loose to drive up the river of herself, did run up as high as the bridge, and broke down some of the rails of the bridge, and so back again with the tide, and up again, and then '''berthed''' himself so well as no pilot could ever have done better; {{...}}}}
### {{lb|en|specifically|space flight}} To [[use#Verb|use]] a [[device]] to bring (a [[spacecraft]]) into its berth or [[dock#Noun|dock]].
## {{lb|en|by extension|chiefly|passive voice}} To [[assign#Verb|assign]] (someone) a berth ''(noun {{senseno|en|vessel bed}} or {{senseno|en|bed}})'' or [[place#Noun|place]] to [[sleep#Verb|sleep]] on a vessel, a [[train#Noun|train]], etc.
## {{lb|en|figurative}} To provide (someone) with a berth ''(noun {{senseno|en|position}})'' or [[appointment]], [[job#Noun|job]], or [[position#Noun|position]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}:
## {{lb|en|reflexive|nautical}} Of a vessel: to [[move#Verb|move]] into a berth.
## {{lb|en|by extension}} Of a person: to [[occupy]] a berth.
##* {{RQ:Stevenson Kidnapped|chapter=I Go to Sea in the Brig ''Covenant'' of Dysart|page=61|passage=The cabin-boy Ransome {{...}} came in at times from the round-house, where he '''berthed''' and served, now nursing a bruised limb in silent agony, now raving against the cruelty of Mr. Shuan.}}

